module
public import CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter15.Section03.CharacteristicTwo.RankOneConfiguration.Recognition.SmallExtension.MarkedMS.AlternatingSixReconstruction.Exceptional.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.Faithful
public import CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterC.Section01.RankTwo.Classification.BinaryCoreBound.MS93.Reduction.SectionThree.NaturalStructure.Exclusion.MovingAbelian.Displacement.Transfer
public import CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterC.Section01.RankTwo.Classification.BinaryCoreBound.MS93.Reduction.SectionThree.NaturalStructure.Exclusion.MovingAbelian.Pairing

/-!
# The actual moving-radius section in the faithful exceptional branch

For the original small MS pair with cubic quotient center and initial
X-action kernel Q, the remaining distance is four. The radius intersection
acts on the endpoint X through the critical commutator. Common critical
images bound its image modulo the midpoint Q by four. These estimates give
fixed codimension at most two on the literal moving central quotient.

The vertex is generated by its residual and the incident radical, which
need not be Sylow and is not replaced by Q. This yields generation of the
radius by its moving subgroup and an abelian incident radius. The fixed
derived subgroup and residual commutator idempotence identify the full and
moving derived subgroups. The resulting central quotient is elementary and
fully moved by the same residual; noncommutation supplies a nonzero
residual-invariant alternating commutator form.

The final section retains the actual critical pair, endpoint actor,
hexacode coordinates and quotient action. It is applied to the whole-core
exhaustion and literal C.1.32 consumers. Excluding this representation,
the cubic larger-distance branch and final reconstruction remain with the
reconstruction owner; no invariant-form vanishing is assumed or asserted.

The geometric and quotient proofs adapt the group-independent arguments in
BinaryCoreBound/MS93/Reduction/SectionThree/NaturalStructure/Exclusion,
using the original radical generation in place of the binary local source.
Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher (1993), (3.8), pp.877--879;
Aschbacher--Smith C.1.31--32, pp.135--136.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section
